{
  "abstract": "A proposed extension of the pair-correlation method for counting simple zeros on the critical line would compare the energy of actual off-line mirror pairs with the clipped energy of virtual doubles. Paper 150 calls the universal comparison the level-two bridge B2 and leaves it open. We give an explicit finite, separated configuration that violates B2 for Knausgård's second window and separation parameter 17/20. It has 168 multiplicity-two objects, 48 of them mirror pairs, with rational heights and depths. The energy margin is rigorously enclosed between -0.15484 and -0.15483. One certificate uses ball arithmetic and a certified eigenvalue solver; a second uses ball-arithmetic residual bounds, a polar decomposition and the Hoffman-Wielandt inequality. A separately coded physical-space quadrature checks the energy formulas. The matched all-on-line configuration has positive margin approximately 7.88874, and the finite counterexample was found by examining a periodic family whose boundary cost hides the violation in smaller cuts. The result rules out this universal bridge as stated, while leaving open weaker or averaged bridges and any exclusion based on additional arithmetic properties of actual zeta zeros. It neither resolves the Riemann hypothesis nor improves a proven zero proportion.",
